      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "ACK 192db92e. The constant-time conversion answers my question, and it costs under 1% against 85ee5f5 at every size I timed, in medians of seven interleaved rounds, so the speedups hold.\n\nThe stock `ctime_tests` cannot see this change: it runs the sender before the context is randomized, and `t_k` is declassified, so nothing secret reaches the conversion. A local probe runs the sender after `secp256k1_context_randomize` with a secret seed. Under Valgrind, 85ee5f5 gives 313 errors from 12 places, eight of them in the variable-time conversion as you describe, and 192db92 none there. Three branches remain, in the infinity check, `fe_normalize_var` and `extrakeys_ge_even_y`. They depend only on whether the output is infinity and on its y, not on the blinding, so they are false positives; a test like the probe would need to declassify the output before them. The outputs still match master and btclib-ecc on 600 random sends (15300 outputs), and the BIP352 vectors pass. Probe and scripts available.\n",
